filsys 1.5.1 (2026-09-06)
=========================

A cleanup and regression release; no on-disk or API change.

- Every -Wsign-conversion warning is fixed by type, not cast: block numbers,
  counts and interleave factors are unsigned throughout, makefree returns
  uint32_t, and uid/gid ownership is uid_t/gid_t end to end.  The remaining
  conversions are explicit POSIX boundary casts (nlink into the stat struct,
  a byte count into truncate's off_t).  The sign changes that produced the
  original isize underflow are now impossible by construction.
- mkfs's sb_put byte-order shims are gone, and its byte order now routes
  through the byte_order_ops table (v7_fmt.bo->put16/get16 for the V7 family,
  bo_me.* for the always-PDP-11 V6/V1/2.11BSD paths) instead of the hardcoded
  bo_put16le/bo_put32me primitives.
- test_matrix grows from a single-size smoke test into a per-format regression
  suite: it writes and reads back odd byte counts, the exact direct-to-indirect
  promotion boundary, and the size-field ceiling, checks the reported size
  matches, and requires fsck errors=0 missing=0 dup=0 after truncate cycles.

filsys 1.5.0 (2026-09-06)
=========================

The "one engine" rework from 1.4.0 is carried to completion: the V1 and PDP-7
backends fold into the shared engine, and the five near-duplicate fsck checkers
collapse into one shared driver.

- The four per-edition *_check() bodies (776 lines across V7/V6/V1/PDP-7) are
  folded into one filsys_check_common() driver (check.c); each edition supplies
  only its maxino/data-start/data-end, inode classifier, block walker, free-list
  walker, makefree and preen.
- The block layer is a data-block codec: blk_get/blk_put move a block as
  logical bytes (bsize) while read_block/write_block move the raw container
  bytes.  Byte-addressed editions share them; the PDP-7's 2-chars-per-18-bit-
  word pack/unpack is the one codec that differs.
- The allocator is a vtable (alloc_ops): the free-list cache (V6/V7/2.11BSD)
  and V1's dual bitmap are two implementations behind balloc/bfree/ialloc/ifree.
- V1's backend state becomes the shared filsys_edition_t (bitmap in a union
  arm, maxino a field); V1's file/dir/lookup layer rides the shared engine,
  leaving only the 32-byte inode codec, the ILARG bmap and the bitmap allocator
  V1-specific.
- PDP-7's state likewise becomes filsys_edition_t (free-list head in
  .freelist); its file/write/lookup layer rides the shared engine, leaving only
  the word container codec, the 8-word dirent and the single-indirect bmap
  PDP-7-specific.

Net: about 1,500 lines removed from the 1.3.2 peak, the longest function is
96 lines, and the test matrix plus fsck still round-trip all eleven formats
with zero errors.

filsys 1.4.0 (2026-09-06)
=========================

The internal architecture is reworked so every edition runs through one engine
instead of five near-duplicate backends:

- The V6 and 2.11BSD backends are folded into the V7 engine: v6fs.c and
  bsd211fs.c are deleted, and their behaviour (V6's s_isize-as-block-count,
  32-byte inode and ILARG bmap; 2.11BSD's 4-byte block addresses and
  variable-length directory entries) now lives as per-edition functions in the
  one v7fs.c.
- A byte-order ops table (byteorder.c) holds the single copy of each
  little-/big-/middle-endian primitive, so a consolidated algorithm calls
  through the descriptor's byte order instead of re-reading fields.
- A format descriptor (filsys_format.c) replaces the ~40 edition comparisons in
  the facade with one per-edition row; filsys_getformat() returns the
  filsys_edition_t that carries both the descriptor and the runtime state.

Net effect is about a thousand fewer lines across eleven byte-identical
formats; the test matrix and fsck still round-trip every edition with zero
errors.

filsys 1.3.2 (2026-09-05)
=========================

- Remove the last fixed-width assumptions from the version-agnostic facade.
  The block size is now a per-edition switch (edition_bsize) instead of a
  ternary that silently omitted 2.11BSD; the truncate zero-fill buffer is sized
  to the largest block size instead of a hardcoded 512 bytes (which would have
  left the upper half of a 1024-byte block stale).  The name-length regression
  now asserts every edition at 14/30/63/64 characters.

filsys 1.3.1 (2026-09-05)
=========================

- Fix 2.11BSD long filenames: the version-agnostic facade (filsys.c) hardcoded
  a 15-byte path-component buffer and reported f_namemax as 14 for every
  edition, so 2.11BSD's variable-length directory entries (63 characters) were
  rejected before they ever reached the backend.  The facade now uses each
  edition's real name limit (8 for V1/PDP-7, 14 for the fixed-width editions,
  63 for 2.11BSD), so -v bsd211 accepts names up to 63 characters.  A
  name-length regression is added to the test matrix.

filsys 1.3.0 (2026-09-05)
=========================

Three V7-derived filesystems join the family, and mkfs/fsck now build and check
every edition:

- 2.11BSD (edition "bsd211"), a new backend (bsd211fs.c) rather than a v7fs
  variant: 1024-byte blocks, the "new" BSD inode with 4-byte block addresses (7
  slots: 4 direct + 3 indirect), a di_flags field, live symbolic links, and
  variable-length directory entries of up to 63 characters (DIRBLKSIZ 512
  framing inside the 1024-byte blocks).  Verified against the 2.11BSD MSCP test
  image.
- Xenix (edition "xenix"): V7 at 1024-byte blocks, little-endian byte order,
  2-byte superblock packing, a 100-entry free cache, and a magic (0x2b5544) at
  superblock offset 1016.  The magic is validated at open to catch a volume
  named -v xenix that is not actually Xenix; it is never used to autodetect the
  edition.
- 2.9BSD (edition "bsd29"): V7 byte-for-byte except for 1024-byte blocks and a
  4+3 address inode (4 direct + 3 indirect instead of V7's 10+3), so it rides
  the v7fs backend.
- The block size is now a runtime property (512 or 1024) threaded through the
  v7fs engine; every pre-existing edition stays at 512, so nothing changes for
  them.
- mkfs.filsys and fsck.filsys now support every edition: the v7fs mkfs path is
  generalized to the three block sizes and per-edition byte order, free-cache
  width, and address count, and bsd211 gets its own mkfs (root + lost+found in
  the variable-length format).
- 32V is renamed vax32, so the edition name no longer begins with a digit (like
  pdp7 for V0); "32v" and "32" remain accepted aliases.
- The filesystem version is now required: mount/fsck/mkfs no longer default to
  v7, and a missing or wrong -v is an error rather than a fallback.

filsys 1.2.7 (2026-09-04)
=========================

- Coherent (Mark Williams Co.) filesystem support across mount, fsck and mkfs
  (edition "coherent"/"coh"/"33").  Coherent is V7 with a 64-entry free-list
  cache, an s_unique field, and an s_m/s_n cylinder interleave, and — unlike
  32V — it keeps the PDP-11's middle-endian byte order (the on-disk format was
  fixed on the PDP-11 and preserved verbatim on x86).  fsck -s and mkfs rebuild
  the free list with the interleave, and mkfs grows -m/-n to set the factors.
  Verified against the 4.2.10 base floppies (disk1..4.4.10.dd).
- Coherent's s_m/s_n free-list interleave (maptab[i] = (i/ratio) + (i%ratio)*m)
  is now honoured by fsck -s and mkfs -m/-n; V7/32V build their free list
  sequentially (m = n = 1), and s_tfree now reflects exactly the free blocks,
  not the sentinel.
- fsck tracks a per-inode state byte (UNALLOC / UNKNOWN / IDIR / IREG / ICHR /
  IBLK / IPIPE, plus IBAD and phase flags) instead of a bare allocation bitmap,
  and flags an inode whose type bits name none of the recognised kinds
  ("unknown type") rather than trusting its block addresses.  Every edition.
- fsck bad-block handling: inode 1 (the V7/Coherent bad-block inode) may
  reference i-list blocks to record gone-bad blocks without being flagged, and
  an out-of-range block reference is counted as a bad block.  More than 10 bad
  blocks (or a bad superblock) aborts the check before the free-list and
  directory phases, so a badly-corrupt image no longer produces a cascade of
  phantom "missing" blocks.
- fsck duplicate-block rescan (phase 1b): a duplicate indirect block is no
  longer chased, so a shared indirect block reports one duplicate rather than
  one per child, and each duplicate is attributed to the inode that first
  claimed the block ("block N dup; inode=M (owner O)").
- fsck interactive repair: -y answers yes to every repair, -i prompts on
  standard input before each ("fix link count ... [yes/no]?"), and -p preens
  without prompting.  This also fixes V7 preen, whose reconnect check skipped
  regular files (V7's IFREG is a non-zero type bit), so orphaned regular files
  are now actually reconnected to lost+found.
- The state byte, bad/dup limits, phase-1b rescan and yes/no/ask prompting are
  the classic BSD fsck design (2.11BSD's sbin/fsck, back to Bill Joy).

filsys 1.2.6 (2026-09-04)
========================

- fsck -p (preen): fixes the safe subset without prompting — unreferenced
  regular files are reconnected to lost+found, link counts are corrected, and
  free-but-referenced inodes are cleared.  Every edition.  V1 and PDP-7, whose
  8-char names are too short for "lost+found", name the recovery directory
  "lostfils".
- fsck dcheck: directory entries are validated against the inode table (a
  directory pointing at a free or out-of-range inode is an error) and link
  counts are cross-checked, folding V7's icheck+dcheck pair into one pass.
- fsck -f: force a check even when the superblock is marked clean.  V6/V7/32V
  carry the s_fmod flag: a read-write mount stamps it dirty and a clean unmount
  clears it, so a bare fsck now skips a clean filesystem.

filsys 1.2.5 (2026-09-04)
========================

- mkfs v1: round the volume up to a multiple of 16 blocks, so the free map (a
  whole number of 16-bit words) exactly describes the image.  Previously a
  size like 100 or 6500 blocks produced a superblock that implied a larger
  filesystem than the file held, and fsck rejected the fresh filesystem with
  EINVAL.
- test_matrix: mkfs_cleanliness() makes a fresh filesystem at several volume
  sizes per format (100/1000/4000/6500 blocks, plus the PDP-7's fixed size)
  and fsck-checks each — the widening that surfaced the V1 sizing bug.

filsys 1.2.4 (2026-09-04)
========================

- fsck: unreferenced-but-unfree ("missing") blocks now count as errors, so a
  damaged filesystem exits non-zero (key for `make check` / CI).
- mkfs v1: rejects a volume whose free/inode bitmaps would not fit in the
  two-block superblock, naming the limit (6528 blocks) instead of overflowing.
- mkfs v0 (PDP-7): rejects a size argument (the RB09 geometry is fixed) instead
  of silently ignoring it.
- fsck/mkfs `-v` edition spellings normalised to `v0|v1|v2|v3|v4|v5|v6|v7|32v`.
- New per-format test matrix (`test_matrix`, run by `make check`) exercises
  write/read/truncate/delete at each format's boundaries, oversized-write
  unwind, and mkfs argument validation — no FUSE or fetched image needed.

filsys 1.2.3 (2026-09-04)
========================

- V6 large files: the small-to-large promotion gap fixed for V1 and PDP-7 in
  1.2.2, now fixed for V6 too (a file past 4096 bytes promoted to ILARG instead
  of failing).
- Failed writes unwind: blocks allocated past the original size are freed and
  the inode reset, then the error propagates, so a failed write leaks nothing
  on any format.
- V1/V6 file-size ceilings corrected to the size-field limit (64 KB / 16 MB),
  not the block-address limit.
- Added the per-format test matrix (`test_matrix`, run by `make check`).

filsys 1.2.2 (2026-09-04)
========================

- PDP-7 large files: small-to-large promotion in the block map.
- Byte-exact sizes: odd-size files no longer round up on the last block.
- Allocators zero a freshly allocated block on every format.

filsys 1.2.1 (2026-09-04)
========================

- fsck `-s` (rebuild the free list) and `-r` (resolve duplicate blocks) now
  cover the PDP-7 and V1 backends, not just V6/V7.
- mkfs builds a 32V filesystem.

filsys 1.2.0 (2026-09-04)
========================

- V1/V2/V3 support: the First through Third Edition share one on-disk format
  (10-byte directory entries, a bitmap free/inode allocator, 32-byte inodes,
  root inode 41), read and written as a single code path.
- PDP-7 (v0) support: the word-addressed PDP-7 filesystem, read-write.
- Replaced the union-plus-ternary dispatch with a per-format ops table
  (`filsys_ops`) that every backend plugs into.
- fsck: ncheck (`-N`) and clri (`-C`) work across every edition; added `-s`
  (salvage) and `-r` (resolve duplicates).
- 32V fsck fixed.

filsys 1.0.0 (2026-08-27)
========================

First stable release: V4, V5, V6, V7 and 32V filesystems, read-write and
read-only, feature complete and tested against real images.

- Mount V4/V5/V6 (identical on disk), V7 (PDP-11) and 32V (VAX) images.
- Middle-endian (PDP-11) and little-endian (VAX) byte-order handling, including
  the VAX 4-byte-aligned superblock field offsets.
- Read/write/mkdir/rmdir/unlink/rename/chmod/chown/truncate/utimens/statfs.
- Free-list and inode-table integrity check with -c.
- Partition mounting with -o offset=, and nested mounts of two partitions of
  one disk.
- findfs.filsys: locate filesystem superblocks (partitions) on a raw image.
- No lock on the image, so several partitions of one disk can be mounted at
  once.
- mkfs.filsys: create a V7 filesystem (superblock, i-list, free list, root
  directory) inside an image, at an optional block offset (-o) and with an
  optional PDP-11 boot block (-b).
- fsck.filsys: check a V7 filesystem's free list and inode table (a thin
  wrapper over the mount driver's -c check).
